<TMU|<tuple|1.1.0|2025.0.15>>

<style|<tuple|generic|number-europe>>

<\body>
  \;

  Want to measure the path integral of free partical(no potential energy) from <math|x<rsub|0> to x<rsub|f>>, which is to measure:

  <\equation>
    <big|int>Dx \<Phi\><around*|[|x|]>,
  </equation>

  here <math|\<Phi\><around*|[|x|]>> is the function of path, <math|Dx> is a measure(explain latter).

  \;

  <section| Examine Dx>

  Assume the x-t trajecture of the partical is piece-wise linear, which means we can use linear function to interpolate the curve in a small time range. Set this partition to the curve <math|<around*|{|x<rsub|0,>x<rsub|1>,\<ldots\>,x<rsub|N>=x<rsub|f>|}>,>

  We get:

  <\equation>
    <big|int><rsub|x<rsub|0>><rsup|x<rsub|f>>Dx=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>\<ldots\><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>d x<rsub|1>\<ldots\>dx<rsub|N-1>.
  </equation>

  In math, I believe <math|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>dx<rsub|1>> means at time <math|t<rsub|1>>, the summation of prob. the partical gets to the range of <math|d x<rsub|1>>. eg: particle at time <math|t<rsub|1>> is in the range of 3-3.1, where <math|d x<rsub|1>> is 3.1-3.

  In this way, we split thie abstract measure into a product measure of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|N-1>> space.

  \;

  <\section>
    Examine <math|\<Phi\><around*|[|x|]>>
  </section>

  Consider the summation energy of particle, which is:

  <\equation>
    S<around*|[|x|]>=<big|int><rsub|t<rsub|0>><rsup|t<rsub|f>>dt<around*|{|<frac|1|2>m<around*|(|<frac|dx|dt>|)><rsup|2>-U<around*|(|x<around*|(|t|)>|)>|}>.
  </equation>

  Since it is free particle, <math|U<around*|(|x|)>=0.> By my knowledge, the prob. of a particle exists in some place is related to its energy, so here we define:

  <\equation>
    \<Phi\><around*|[|x|]>=e<rsup|<wide|i|^><frac|S<around*|[|x|]>|\<hbar\>>>.
  </equation>

  <section|Exact Computation>

  Since we assurm piece-wise linear, which means <math|x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i+1>> the particle is in constant velosity, hence (3) can be simplified to:

  <\equation>
    S<around*|[|x|]>=<frac|m|2\<Delta\>t><around*|(|<around*|(|x<rsub|1>-x<rsub|0>|)><rsup|2>+\<cdots\>+<around*|(|x<rsub|f>-x<rsub|N-1>|)><rsup|2>|)>.
  </equation>

  Combine (2),(5), if Fubini's theorem is workable, we can interchange the integral to get the product of <math|N-1> integral:

  <\equation>
    <big|int><rsub|x<rsub|0>><rsup|x<rsub|f>>Dx \<Phi\><around*|[|x|]>=<below|\<Pi\>|i\<in\><around*|[|N-1|]>><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>>dx<rsub|i><frac|m|2\<Delta\>t><around*|(|x<rsub|i>-x<rsub|i-1>|)><rsup|2>\<times\><frac|m|2\<Delta\>t><around*|(|x<rsub|f>-x<rsub|N-1>|)><rsup|2>.
  </equation>

  Not hard to find each integral is a Gaussian Integral, which solves the problem.

  <\remark>
    From the result of Gaussian Integral we may have the intuition that we are actually measuring each linear path of particle under Gaussian measure, where I believe in the theory of Gaussian process we have the similar way to do so. Anyway, we successfully transverse a functional integral into a integral on Euclidean space!
  </remark>
</body>

